WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.310
So who is my guest this week?

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:05.850
The Grand Slam tennis
champion, Andy Murray.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.090
The career span of an
elite athlete can be short,

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.710
but this British tennis player

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.430
has already packed a hell
of a lot into his 35 years,

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.550
three Grand Slam wins and
a former world number one,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.220
he's battled injury to
remain on the court,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.430
bagging tens of millions
of dollars along the way.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.340
But for the greatest UK
player since Virginia Wade,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.100
it's always been about
more than the money.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.580
He's turned down endorsements
and done things his own way.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.630
From his early years,

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.880
when some of the public
were unsure of him,

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.740
he's now ranked by pollsters

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.250
as the most popular tennis
player in the world.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.100
As he faces what could
be his last Wimbledon,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.780
what does he do next?

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.000
And can he avoid the pitfalls that occur

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.333
for so many top sports players
once they've quit their game?

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.600
This is what's coming up.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.030
I know we're talking ahead
of Wimbledon, of course.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.450
Do you have to go in
believing you can win again?

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.120
Yeah, I mean, I think that's
part of the motivation

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.980
to still be out there competing.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.880
I know it's gonna be even
harder to win Wimbledon

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.550
than it was five, six years ago,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.390
but yeah, I still believe,
and I'm still working

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.910
and training as hard as I can

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.770
to try and try and achieve that goal.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.200
You've been very influenced
by strong women in your life,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.640
not least your mum,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.340
the pro tennis coach,
of course, Judy Murray,

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.840
and she recently wrote an article

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.390
about being sexually
assaulted at an awards dinner

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.100
by a man sat next to her.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.800
I was obviously really upset for her.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.363
I was pretty angry, but also,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.070
I didn't know quite exactly what to say.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.830
It's a familiar story for a lot of women,

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.500
and yeah, that sort of behavior

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.663
shouldn't be tolerated anywhere.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.540
Andy Murray, hello.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.490
Thank you very much for talking to me.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:06.330
Hi, how are you doing?

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.240
Do people call you Sir Andy, ever?

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.440
Rarely, no.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.101
I try to avoid that when I can.

00:02:12.101 --> 00:02:13.890
Okay, no, no, I mean, you
are, you are a knight,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.300
so I just was remembering that.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.370
Do do your kids ever call you it?

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.140
I don't think they're aware of it.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.633
They have started calling me Andy Murray,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.800
which I find pretty awkward.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.910
They tend to do it when they're
around their other friends

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.530
to wind me up.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.300
Well, it sounds like
you've taught them well,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.650
to wind you up already.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.840
I love that, Andy Murray,
from your children.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.750
I know we're talking ahead
of Wimbledon, of course,

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.960
do you have to go in
believing you can win again?

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.690
Yeah, I mean, I think that's
part of the motivation

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.520
to still be out there competing.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.830
I know, obviously, that's gonna be

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.630
an unbelievably difficult thing to do,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:56.630
but I do still believe that
I've got great tennis in me.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.000
I know it's gonna be even harder

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.873
to win Wimbledon than it
was five, six years ago,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.030
but yeah, I still believe,
and I'm still working

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.580
and training as hard as I can

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.440
to try and achieve that goal.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.940
Yes, well, I mean, we should say,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:15.810
in case people don't know,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:19.740
you've had a lot of pain, injuries,

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.570
particularly around your hip,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.170
and I read that you're inspired
by the likes of Muhammad Ali

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.110
and sports stars who make big comebacks.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.120
Is that part of the psychology?

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.330
For me, I came back to play
tennis because I love it.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:37.350
That's why I came back to play.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.160
It's been something I've done
since I was a really young kid

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.700
and pretty much since my
sort of teenage years,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.370
it's been the main thing in my life,

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.407
and I've dedicated a lot of time to it,

00:03:49.407 --> 00:03:50.610
and I kind of realized

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.187
when I was going through
all of the injuries and pain

00:03:53.187 --> 00:03:57.030
and wasn't able to compete
how much I did love it.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.180
And yeah, probably wish, at times,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.410
certainly earlier in my career

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.720
that I appreciated it a bit more

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.270
and enjoyed it a little
bit more than I did.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.730
Yes, well, I mean, I'm fascinated

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.370
by how individuals do cope with pain.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.430
I actually live with a
condition called endometriosis,

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.800
so I'm quite regularly in pain.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.200
Of course, everyone's pain
is very subjective to them.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.410
And in your case, it
stopped you at many moments

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.930
from doing what you
wanted, what you dream of,

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:25.920
what you train of.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.080
How have you coped with pain?

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.660
Well, I think as athletes,

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:34.620
you're used to sort of always
having a certain level of pain

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.190
from the training and
the matches that you do,

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:39.270
and that's kind of normal.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.790
So I think most athletes probably have

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.130
a relatively high sort of pain tolerance,

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.610
but then with my hip,

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.710
and I dealt with discomfort in my hip

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.720
for probably six or seven years,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.683
until it, basically, from one
day to the next went from,

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.370
I was ranked number one
in the world at the time

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.820
to I literally can't
walk properly anymore,

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.390
and I couldn't extend my
hip and was in constant pain

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.330
every time, even when I
went to walk the dogs.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.420
And I think, initially,

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.670
that's obviously difficult to deal with,

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.580
but I think over time, it's
the consistency of the pain

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.290
and it just being every single day

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.380
that sort of wore me down.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.120
And I filmed a documentary
during my sort of rehab

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.940
and all the injuries and
the surgeries and stuff,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.870
and when I watched that
back, I kind of realized,

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.330
which I didn't whilst
I was going through it,

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:37.620
I was in a pretty bad place,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.510
so probably didn't deal
with it particularly well

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.490
in the end, but it had been
going on for a long time

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.847
and it was stopping me from
doing what I love as well.

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:50.940
And I cannot wait to see
you back at Wimbledon.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.203
I really, really can't.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.530
I did think about, though,
when you were in that state,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.620
and I know about that film,
I've watched film that you made,

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.660
I was thinking about whether
you'd got to a place,

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.310
but maybe it's premature to ask you this,

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.880
but have you thought about
how you will be happy

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.458
or satisfied if you never
win another Grand Slam?

00:06:09.458 --> 00:06:12.060
Yeah, I mean, look,

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.450
I don't expect that to happen.

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.190
I still believe that I can do it,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:22.190
but I got to a point
before I had the operation

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.390
that I had to contemplate
not playing tennis again,

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.970
and I was fighting and
battling with that as well

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.120
during the injury troubles.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.870
And I knew that when I
had the hip resurfacing,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.910
that there was a chance I might
not be able to play again,

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:40.710
and I had to come to terms with that.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:45.120
Once I did, I feel fine.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.550
I know that I'll be fine

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.653
if I had to stop playing tennis tomorrow,

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.960
I'll be absolutely fine.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.370
I've got a great family.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.800
I would obviously miss playing tennis,

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.970
but I have lots to look forward to

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.080
away from the tennis court.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.420
Well, you're a fashion
designer now, right?

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.120
You've just been doing a
photo shoot, I believe,

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.530
a model and a fashion designer,

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:10.710
your sportswear range, Andy
Murray, let me get this right,

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:14.370
Andy Murray x Castore, that's
your new calling, right?

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.470
Yeah, so I mean,

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.143
I wouldn't call myself a fashion designer.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:23.520
I don't know a whole lot
about fashion, to be honest,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.400
but I try and get
involved as much as I can

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.320
in terms of the way the clothes
perform on the tennis court.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.370
That's something that interests me a lot,

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:35.520
so I try and help with that.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.230
I test a lot of the kit and everything

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.150
before I, obviously, have to perform on it

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.770
and give feedback and
try to make improvements

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.670
to where I can, but yeah,

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.620
I let the fashion designers take care

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:47.910
of the fashion side of things

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:51.480
because I don't know a
whole lot about that.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.270
We can't underestimate
the power of clothes,

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:55.420
and for you, I suppose,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:57.750
it's something you wrap yourself in.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:00.000
It's a bit of an armor at the same time.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.430
Yeah, I mean, it is
really important for us,

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.203
and the shoes as well.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.360
It can make a difference
to your performance,

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.500
and certainly, the clothes
and extreme conditions,

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.500
we play in Australia
where it's 40 degrees,

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:21.180
and in places like Miami

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.990
where it can be 85, 90% humidity,

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.690
so you need to make
sure that the materials

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:30.900
and stuff that you're
wearing are appropriate

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.433
for the conditions.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.820
So, yeah, it is important.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.470
I just have to say, I
talked about watching you,

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.360
of course, as a Brit,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.900
I feel I've lived many
moments with you on my knees,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.210
standing up, hiding behind the sofa,

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:48.570
willing you on to win,
but one of the things

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:51.900
that I wanted to ask you was
about your kind of fist pump

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.740
that you do, and that roar
that you do to the crowd,

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:56.670
what's going on there?

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:57.930
What are you thinking about

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.420
when people like me are
watching you do that,

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.490
point after point after point?

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:03.900
Yeah, I mean, I think it's just,

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:06.480
well, a little bit is, obviously,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.373
trying to encourage yourself,

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:11.160
but then, yeah, there's moments in a match

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.060
where you're trying to
engage with the crowd

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:17.640
that are coming there to watch the tennis,

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.900
hopefully be entertained,

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.390
but to support one of the
two players out there.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:25.290
And when people come to
watch sporting events,

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.633
they want to see that emotion.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:32.760
Hopefully, it's positive emotion,

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:37.293
but sometimes, for me,
it's negative as well.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.700
I wish I was able to control
the negative emotions

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:43.620
that I have when I'm playing better,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.740
but I feel like when I do it to the crowd,

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.680
and fist pump and get
excited on the court,

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.983
that I'm trying to engage the crowd.

00:09:51.983 --> 00:09:54.390
No, no, and it's great to,
of course, have crowds back

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.580
and feed off that energy.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:00.150
What do you make of groaning
and grunting whilst playing?

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:01.140
What do you make of that?

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.840
Some spectators find that not the best,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.820
but I wonder how it is as a player.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:06.930
And other spectators love it.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.900
I remember growing up watching
Monica Seles, for instance.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.300
There's a certain level
that I think is okay,

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:14.833
and almost natural when
you're putting lots of effort

00:10:14.833 --> 00:10:18.180
into something, but when it's at a level

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.540
that sounds and seems unnatural,

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:24.690
then that's when it's maybe
a little bit off putting,

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:28.380
and then some players are
maybe doing it so long

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:32.103
that the ball's already
getting hit by the opponent,

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:37.290
whilst they're grunting,
and that is not good

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:40.890
'cause it should be a hindrance, really.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.560
Yes, it's not their ball
very much at that moment.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:45.360
No, I can only imagine.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.520
Well, we're just speaking
about, of course, again,

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.550
with regards to Wimbledon ahead of it,

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:52.860
you will be very aware of the controversy

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:54.780
going into this Wimbledon
surrounding the decision

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.960
to ban Russian and Belarusian players.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.780
You've acknowledged how
complicated this decision is.

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:02.100
You chose your words very carefully,

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:05.160
and you have sympathy for
the players who can't play,

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.637
and the authorities who have done this.

00:11:07.637 --> 00:11:10.020
It's a really difficult
one, this, isn't it?

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.810
Well, my sympathies, first and foremost,

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:14.880
are with the people in Ukraine,

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.430
and what they're going through.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:20.700
In the grand scheme of things,

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.370
whether some tennis players
are not able to play

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:24.450
in an event or not,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.930
and whether there's
ranking points on offer,

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:31.110
in the grand scheme of
things, is kind of irrelevant.

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.450
Obviously, I know quite a
few of the Russian players

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:35.670
and I'm friends with
them, and I feel for them

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:37.200
that they're not able to compete.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:40.110
I can appreciate that
may seem unfair to them,

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:43.830
but I can also see Wimbledon's side,

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:46.410
and their point of view and
the perspective that they have,

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.623
and it's very complex,
but at the end of it,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.950
there's a war going on.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:56.820
That's what people should be focusing on,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.600
and we want that to end
as quickly as possible,

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:04.800
and a tennis tournament
isn't really that important,

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:05.633
I don't think.

00:12:05.633 --> 00:12:08.430
No, I think that's obviously the context

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.650
and a very important one to you,

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:12.960
and I know you're an
ambassador for UNICEF.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.750
I know you're also donating the proceeds

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:18.270
of any of your payments
or winnings this year

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.040
to the effort to support children,

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:22.020
in particular, in Ukraine.

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.300
But I think what's very
striking about you, Andy,

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:27.630
is you have been outspoken
about a number of issues

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.150
over the years, before, almost,

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:31.050
not that it was fashionable,

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:34.650
but people in sport
perhaps taking positions,

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:37.020
whether it's about the mental
health of young players

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:39.810
to sexism in sport, as well,

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.820
is something I know you care deeply about.

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.340
Do you get more confident
to share opinions

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:46.830
as you get older, or less?

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:49.770
Does it become easier or harder?

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.106
I think in some ways it becomes easier

00:12:52.106 --> 00:12:55.200
because you're maybe, I don't know,

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:56.670
when you're in your early 20s,

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:59.460
you don't really know much
about anything, really.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.680
And I'm not saying I know loads now,

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:04.650
but I know a lot more now than I did then,

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:09.450
and I maybe trust my
views a little bit better

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:10.290
than I did then.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:15.290
And I also feel as well,
like since I had children,

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:19.290
that I want to make sure
that I support things

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:20.520
that are important to me,

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:23.490
and that hopefully will
maybe help the world

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.580
that my children grow up in as well.

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.820
So in some ways, yes, it does,

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:31.740
maybe it does help as you get older,

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.350
and maybe it becomes a bit
easier to share your opinions,

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:35.553
but at the same time,

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.960
with everything that
goes on online nowadays,

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.930
and everything like online abuse,

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:46.567
and those sorts of things,
at times, you think,

00:13:46.567 --> 00:13:47.550
"Well, what's the point?

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:51.870
I'll just say nothing
because it's an easier life

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:52.800
that way as well."

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:57.570
And I wanna focus all my
time and energy on my family

00:13:57.570 --> 00:14:01.110
and on my tennis, and
when you do get involved

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:04.683
in certain topics and subjects,

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:09.114
it attracts more attention,
more questions, more time,

00:14:09.114 --> 00:14:13.590
more comments online, and that
can be quite tiring as well.

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:15.600
Yes, well, I mean, you
mentioned your family there,

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:17.700
of course, as well as
three daughters and a son,

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.100
you've been very influenced
by strong women in your life,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.470
not least your mum, the pro tennis coach,

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:24.240
of course, Judy Murray.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:25.770
And she recently wrote an article,

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:29.190
and I actually remember
reading it when it came out,

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.880
and it's a terribly shocking,

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.850
but horribly familiar story
to quite a lot of women,

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:37.110
I have to say.

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:40.320
She wrote about being sexually
assaulted at an awards dinner

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:42.690
by a man sat next to her.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:45.840
I just wonder, did you
know already that story,

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.570
and did she tell you she
was going to share it?

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:52.830
My brother was aware of that, I wasn't.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:55.890
My mum did message me at
the time to let me know

00:14:55.890 --> 00:15:00.333
that there was an article
coming out about it.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:04.803
And yeah, I was obviously
really upset for her.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:08.910
I was pretty angry,

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:13.910
but also I didn't know
quite exactly what to say.

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:16.080
And obviously, I messaged my mum,

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.730
I called her the following
day to talk to her

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.140
to make sure that she was okay,

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:22.110
and yeah, like you say,

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:25.350
it's, obviously, a familiar
story for a lot of women,

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.440
and yeah, that sort of behavior

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:30.240
shouldn't be tolerated anywhere.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.730
I mean, she also wrote as part of this,

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:35.947
which is a big part of
what your mum has done, is,

00:15:35.947 --> 00:15:37.650
"Women are made to feel

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:39.630
the world of sport is not for us,

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:42.870
and our presence is neither
welcome nor important."

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:44.670
And what I mean by that is
your mum has done a lot of work

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.350
to try and change that,

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:48.663
to be a big part of the
world of sport she is.

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.590
I wonder, when you have
thought about sexism,

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:53.850
and how women are perceived,

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:55.680
you've, of course, also had
a female coach yourself,

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.090
that was a big story at the time,

00:15:57.090 --> 00:16:00.240
even though it shouldn't
have been in many ways,

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.460
how do you stop those men?

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:04.080
It's not all men in sport, of course,

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:06.450
but those men in sport making women feel

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:07.953
like your mum described?

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:10.170
That's a good question,

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:13.560
which I probably don't
know all of the answers to.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.870
I think some of it probably
comes from education,

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:20.190
some of it probably comes
from having more women around

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:24.033
so that it's not something
that it seems out of the norm.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:28.950
Certainly, I, like you mentioned,

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:32.520
I got a taste of that when
I hired a female coach,

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:34.530
and that was very rare.

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:36.660
On the men's tour and the women's tour,

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:40.320
there's very few women coaches,

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:42.930
and I didn't think much of it at the time

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:44.553
when I hired a female coach.

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:48.660
I'd never personally in my
early 20s thought about it

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.570
'cause I'd always seen
all of the top players

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:53.820
all working with male coaches.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:56.700
I had a conversation with Darren Cahill,

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:58.927
who's one of the top
Australian coaches and he said,

00:16:58.927 --> 00:17:01.650
"Well, what about speaking
to some female coaches?"

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:02.580
And I was like, "Yeah."

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:03.840
I mean, why not?

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:05.820
Why wouldn't I do that?

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:08.790
I then went down the route
of employing Amélie Mauresmo,

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:11.880
who is a former world number
one Wimbledon champion,

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:15.870
and even when there was rumors

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:17.610
about me potentially working with her,

00:17:17.610 --> 00:17:21.030
the comments that I was
getting from fellow players,

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:24.360
fellow coaches, was something
I'd never experienced before,

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:25.590
and that was when I realized

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:27.660
that there was quite a big problem.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.360
And I think that until
it becomes the norm,

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:34.740
that there is more than just the odd one

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:39.720
or two female coaches on
the tour of 200 or 300,

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:43.527
I think that it's probably
always gonna be an issue,

00:17:43.527 --> 00:17:46.710
but I mean, hopefully,
things are changing slowly.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:49.890
Yes, I mean, I suppose when
we look at the world of sport,

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:51.420
and I know I'm broadening
this out slightly,

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:52.740
but I know you will have seen this,

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:54.660
that recently Jake Daniels

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:58.080
became the first active
footballer to come out as gay.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.120
A lot of people have
said that was very brave

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.670
because of football, and
how football is perceived,

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:05.190
and how it is to be a footballer,

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:07.920
when it comes to being
open about your sexuality.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:10.620
I wonder, when we talk
about the world of sport,

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:12.060
what was your reaction to that?

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.910
And do you think it is getting better

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:18.390
in being more reflective
of the wider population

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:21.180
and not the world it
thinks it should present,

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:22.533
but the world as it is?

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:25.770
I do think it is getting a bit better.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:27.450
And I think that that was really positive

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:29.940
that he felt comfortable
enough to come out,

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:31.620
and from what I've seen,

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:35.050
most of the coverage

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:39.790
has been extremely positive about it

00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:42.483
in the media.

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:46.620
How that will play out at football grounds

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:47.580
up and down the country,

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.400
that will be, I guess, a bigger test.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:54.183
And hopefully his experience
is really positive as well.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.573
But yeah, you would think that,

00:18:58.830 --> 00:18:59.760
certainly in tennis,

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:04.410
there's been no male tennis
players have come out as gay

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:06.110
whilst they've still been playing.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:12.150
A few have come out post career,

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:16.230
so we maybe have to ask the question

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:17.580
as to why that is the case

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:21.900
and why they still don't feel comfortable

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:24.000
to come out whilst they're still active,

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:27.780
but I would hope that it
would be viewed positively

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.900
by all of the players,

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:34.080
and that's certainly been
the case on the female side.

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:36.240
So I didn't actually know that
about male tennis players.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.310
I haven't looked into that,

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:39.270
but that's also interesting.

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.550
I mean, is it, particularly
behind the scenes,

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:42.383
I don't know when you're on tour,

00:19:42.383 --> 00:19:45.510
is there a lot of bravado
and having to be macho,

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:47.880
and I say that in inverted commas,

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:49.280
sort of being one way?

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:52.170
Do you think that's what it's
about, the conformity perhaps?

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:56.760
I haven't seen it in terms of sexuality.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:20:00.843
I think in sport, there is always,

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:04.590
well, certainly in male sport,

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:08.130
about being tough and being a real man,

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:10.830
and not showing any emotions
and those sorts of things,

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:13.710
and whether something like
that would play a part in it,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:16.650
I've certainly found it difficult at times

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:20.700
earlier in my career to open
up to the coaches and stuff

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:23.550
that were around me because, I don't know,

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:27.150
showing emotions and these
sorts of things were always seen

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:31.530
as kind of being a weakness, you know?

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:34.680
So I've seen it in that sense,

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:38.760
but not in the locker rooms
around someone's sexuality.

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:39.593
Yeah, but I mean,

00:20:39.593 --> 00:20:41.760
you've also spoken a
lot about mental health

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:43.920
and how younger players, in particular,

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:47.790
do need more support, especially
to try and be themselves

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.340
when they suddenly find
themselves in the spotlight.

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:51.900
You mentioned, there, emotions

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:54.150
and how you were early on in your career.

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.280
I did spy just before we started to talk

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:59.520
a recent Instagram post of
you smiling very broadly,

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:00.510
holding a cup of coffee,

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:02.820
saying "After years of being miserable

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:03.960
and incapable of smiling,

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:07.830
I wish I'd known that all
I needed was a coffee."

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:10.050
Is it fun owning that view,

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:11.160
some people had a view,

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:14.220
that you didn't really
seem that happy at first?

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.793
Well, I wasn't that happy.

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:20.970
I was happy in certain aspects,

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:24.393
but I didn't feel comfortable
in front of a camera.

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:30.570
My life changed almost overnight

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:32.950
from the first time I played in Wimbledon

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:36.600
as an 18-year-old, and
got to the third round,

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:38.340
and I wasn't ready for that.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:39.810
I wasn't prepared for that.

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.213
It's difficult, I think,
to prepare anyone,

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.950
to have that, to sort of no
one in the streets knowing you,

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:50.460
and you're just you just playing tennis

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.320
and playing in front of 10, 15 people,

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:55.350
to, all of a sudden,
playing on the Center Court

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:59.310
at Wimbledon with millions
of people watching on the TV,

00:21:59.310 --> 00:22:02.610
and getting followed by
paparazzi back to your house,

00:22:02.610 --> 00:22:04.080
and those sorts of things,

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:07.620
and seeing your face on
papers and everything,

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:12.120
and comments and things
that you might have said

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:14.730
to your friends that you
maybe made the mistake

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:18.990
of saying in front of a camera,

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:22.140
all of a sudden you
were getting sent abuse,

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:24.060
people saying stuff to
you up in the streets

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:26.160
and these sorts of things,
and it is really difficult.

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:29.790
And you, then, would I think, naturally

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:32.040
go into your shell and
not want to open up,

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:35.580
and just put up a bit of a shield.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:38.190
And that's certainly what I did,

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:42.630
and was not that happy
being in front of a camera,

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:43.980
and did not feel comfortable in it.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:48.810
And it's my responsibility how I deal

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:51.600
with that side of things,

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:55.740
but yeah, I don't think
the media helped me

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:56.640
through that period,

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:59.490
and I don't think that
probably enough is done

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:01.920
to help young athletes prepare themselves

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:06.920
for the spotlight at such a young age.

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:10.623
I am praying that you're
going to win Wimbledon.

00:23:11.613 --> 00:23:13.017
Thank you, me too.

00:23:13.017 --> 00:23:15.330
Are you praying or are you training?

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:16.260
A bit of both?

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:18.840
I'm training, I don't pray.

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:21.600
Okay, I'm not sure I've
put it quite into prayer,

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:23.817
but I'm really hoping,
let's put it like that.

00:23:23.817 --> 00:23:26.340
And when you roar with each fist pump,

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:28.260
I'll be thinking of what you
were saying about all of this,

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:29.850
and it's a pleasure to
talk to you, Andy Murray.

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:30.780
Thank you so much.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:32.127
Thank you very much.

00:23:32.127 --> 00:23:34.440
And thank you for being with us.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:36.723
Until next time, stay safe and goodbye.

